BT /F2 20 Tf 72 720 Td (Chunking in Retrieval-Augmented Generation) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 685 Td (Retrieval-augmented generation answers a question in two steps. First it finds passages) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 670 Td (that look relevant, then it asks a language model to answer from those passages alone.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 655 Td (The passages are chunks: pieces of the original documents, cut before anything is) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 640 Td (indexed. How they are cut decides what the retriever can ever find.) Tj ET
BT /F2 15 Tf 72 606 Td (Why chunk boundaries matter) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 577 Td (A retriever scores each chunk as a whole. When a boundary falls in the middle of an) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 562 Td (explanation, the question lands on one half and the answer on the other, and neither) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 547 Td (half scores well on its own. The model then receives a passage that mentions the topic) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 532 Td (but never resolves it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 508 Td (Boundaries also decide what travels together. A definition separated from the term it) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 493 Td (defines, or a table separated from its caption, loses the context that made it useful.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 478 Td (The embedding of such a chunk describes a fragment, not an idea, so it drifts away from) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 463 Td (the questions it should match.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 439 Td (A useful rule of thumb: a chunk should answer one question well. If you cannot say in a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 424 Td (sentence what a chunk is about, a retriever will struggle to decide when it is relevant,) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 409 Td (and a reader will struggle to use it.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 385 Td (Bad boundaries are hard to see from the final answer. The model is fluent either way, so) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 370 Td (the symptom is usually an answer that is vague or quietly wrong. Looking at the chunks) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 355 Td (themselves, before indexing, is the quickest way to catch the problem.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 331 Td (Chunking is usually done once, when documents are added, and then forgotten. That is a) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 316 Td (pity, because it is one of the few settings that changes what every later step can do.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 292 Td (The examples in this primer use English prose, but the same ideas hold for any language.) Tj ET
BT /F1 11 Tf 72 277 Td (Only the numbers change, because words and tokens have different lengths.) Tj ET
